          "question": "I get chest discomfort only when I am anxious, never on exertion. How do I tell the difference, and what does guidance describe about assessing it?",
          "answer": "If you experience chest discomfort, it is important to seek medical advice, especially if your symptoms become severe, worsen, or are new and unexplained.\n\nUnderstanding chest discomfort can be complex, particularly when distinguishing between anxiety-related symptoms and potential cardiovascular issues. Anxiety can lead to physical symptoms such as chest tightness, palpitations, and breathlessness, which often occur during heightened anxiety or panic (Generalized Anxiety Disorder, General Clinical Reference). However, if your chest discomfort includes pressure or tightness, spreads to other areas like the arms, neck, or back, or is accompanied by symptoms such as breathlessness or light-headedness, it should be viewed as a potential heart attack and prompt immediate medical attention (Emergency Medicine and Cardiology Society Guidance).\n\nIn general, anxiety-related chest discomfort typically arises during episodes of stress rather than during physical exertion, which contrasts with angina or heart-related discomfort that can be triggered by activity (Coronary Artery Disease, General Clinical Reference). Assessing your symptoms may involve evaluating factors such as caffeine and alcohol use, as well as occurrences of panic attacks (Generalized Anxiety Disorder, General Clinical Reference). \n\nIf you are unsure or if there are risk factors associated with cardiovascular disease involved, it is advisable to discuss your symptoms with a clinician to ensure a thorough evaluation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
